---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Disclaimer from '../../components/Disclaimer.astro';
import { states, kits, isEnacted, priceFor, yieldFor, prices, yields, effectiveDate, fmtDate, BUILD_DATE, assumptions } from '../../lib/data';
import { scenario, fmtUsd, fmtYears } from '../../lib/calc';

// Worked example: a real kit from our data (EcoFlow STREAM Microinverter + 2 x 400 W panels).
const ex = kits.find((k) => k.slug === 'ecoflow-stream-microinverter-2x400w')!;
const W = ex.panel_w_total as number;
const COST = ex.price_usd as number;

const sc = assumptions.self_consumption;
const TYP = sc.typical_pct;
type Case = { typ: ReturnType<typeof scenario>; best: ReturnType<typeof scenario> } | null;
const rows = states.filter(isEnacted).sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name)).map((s) => {
  const price = priceFor(s.code);
  const y = yieldFor(s.code);
  const run = (yy: number | 'unknown'): Case => (typeof yy === 'number' && typeof price === 'number' ? { typ: scenario(W, yy, price, TYP, COST), best: scenario(W, yy, price, 100, COST) } : null);
  return { s, price, ideal: run(y.ideal), vertical: run(y.vertical) };
});
const ideals = rows.filter((r) => r.ideal);
const fastest = [...ideals].sort((a, b) => a.ideal!.typ.paybackYears! - b.ideal!.typ.paybackYears!)[0];
const slowest = [...ideals].sort((a, b) => b.ideal!.typ.paybackYears! - a.ideal!.typ.paybackYears!)[0];
// vertical columns appear only when every state in the table has the figure (no "pending" cells)
const showVertical = rows.length > 0 && rows.every((r) => r.vertical);
// Railing (vertical) figures for the short answer, all computed from the data.
const ratioPct = (code: string) => {
  const y = yieldFor(code);
  return typeof y.ideal === 'number' && typeof y.vertical === 'number' ? Math.round((y.vertical / y.ideal) * 100) : null;
};
const ratios = rows.map((r) => ratioPct(r.s.code)).filter((x): x is number => x !== null);
const verts = rows.filter((r) => r.vertical);
const vFast = [...verts].sort((a, b) => a.vertical!.typ.paybackYears! - b.vertical!.typ.paybackYears!)[0];
const vSlow = [...verts].sort((a, b) => b.vertical!.typ.paybackYears! - a.vertical!.typ.paybackYears!)[0];
const cell = (c: NonNullable<Case>['typ']) => `${fmtUsd(c.dollars)} a year · ${fmtYears(c.paybackYears!)}`;
const articleLd = { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'Article', headline: 'Is plug-in solar worth it? Real numbers by state', dateModified: prices.last_checked };
---
<Base title="Is plug-in solar worth it? Real numbers by state" description={`What a ${W} W plug-in solar kit could save in each state with a plug-in law, using EIA electricity prices and NREL solar data, and how long it takes to pay back.`} crumbs={[{ name: 'Guides', href: '/guides/' }, { name: 'Is it worth it?', href: '/guides/is-it-worth-it/' }]} jsonLd={[articleLd]}>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Is plug-in solar worth it?</h1>
    <p style="font-size:1.1rem">It depends on three numbers: what you pay for electricity, how much sun your panels get, and what the kit costs. Here they are for each state with a plug-in law.</p>

    <h2>The short answer</h2>
    {fastest && slowest ? (
      <p>For an {W} W kit costing {fmtUsd(COST)}, with panels tilted toward the sun and a typical share of the power used at home ({TYP}%), payback ranges from about <strong>{(fastest.ideal!.typ.paybackYears!).toFixed(1)} years in {fastest.s.name}</strong> to about <strong>{(slowest.ideal!.typ.paybackYears!).toFixed(1)} years in {slowest.s.name}</strong>. If you used every kilowatt-hour yourself (the best case), it would be {fastest.ideal!.best.paybackYears!.toFixed(1)} and {slowest.ideal!.best.paybackYears!.toFixed(1)} years. The gap between states is mostly the price of electricity: {fastest.s.name} averages {fastest.price}¢ per kWh, {slowest.s.name} {slowest.price}¢.</p>
    ) : <p>We are still adding solar data for these states.</p>}
    {showVertical && vFast && vSlow && ratios.length > 0 && (
      <p>Panels hung flat on a balcony railing produce about {Math.min(...ratios)}–{Math.max(...ratios)}% of what tilted panels make in these states, so at the typical share payback stretches to about <strong>{vFast.vertical!.typ.paybackYears!.toFixed(1)} years in {vFast.s.name}</strong> and <strong>{vSlow.vertical!.typ.paybackYears!.toFixed(1)} years in {vSlow.s.name}</strong>. <a href="/guides/railing-vs-tilted/">Railing vs tilted panels, state by state</a>.</p>
    )}

    <h2>Our example</h2>
    <p>We use a real product from our kit list: the <a href={`/kits/${ex.slug}/`}>{ex.brand} {ex.model}</a>, {W} W of panels, listed at {fmtUsd(COST)} on {fmtDate(ex.price_checked_on!)}. We assume you earn nothing for power sent to the grid: most of these laws exclude plug-in systems from net metering, and the rest do not promise any payment. So what matters is how much of the output you use at home:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Typical ({TYP}%)</strong>: from HTW Berlin's plug-in solar model for an 800 W system without a battery, in a home using 3,000 kWh a year. That is well below every US state's average household use, so most homes will use at least this share.</li>
      <li><strong>Best case (100%)</strong>: you use every kilowatt-hour yourself, for example because something always runs in the daytime.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Panels are assumed tilted at 20° facing south, as on a sunny patio{showVertical ? ', with a second case for panels hung vertically on a south-facing railing' : ''}.</p>
  </article>

  <div class="table-wrap" style="margin:1rem 0">
    <table class="stack">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">Yearly savings and payback for a {W} W kit costing {fmtUsd(COST)}</caption>
      <thead><tr>
        <th scope="col">State</th><th scope="col">Price</th>
        <th scope="col">Tilted: typical ({TYP}%)</th><th scope="col">Tilted: best case (100%)</th>
        {showVertical && <><th scope="col">Railing: typical ({TYP}%)</th><th scope="col">Railing: best case (100%)</th></>}
      </tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {rows.filter((r) => r.ideal).map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href={`/states/${r.s.slug}/`}>{r.s.name}</a>{r.s.verification === 'secondary_only' && <span class="small muted"><br />law being verified</span>}</th>
            <td data-label="Price" class="num">{typeof r.price === 'number' ? `${r.price}¢/kWh` : 'Unknown'}</td>
            <td data-label={`Tilted, typical (${TYP}%)`} class="num">{cell(r.ideal!.typ)}</td>
            <td data-label="Tilted, best case" class="num">{cell(r.ideal!.best)}</td>
            {showVertical && <><td data-label={`Railing, typical (${TYP}%)`} class="num">{cell(r.vertical!.typ)}</td><td data-label="Railing, best case" class="num">{cell(r.vertical!.best)}</td></>}
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <article class="prose">
    <h2>What makes it better or worse</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Your electricity price.</strong> This matters most. Check your bill's price per kWh, including delivery charges, and use it in the <a href="/#tool">calculator</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Using the power yourself.</strong> Savings come from power you use while the sun shines: the fridge, a router, things on standby. Power sent back to the grid usually earns nothing. Vermont's law says so directly. Connecticut's law requires devices to warn that exports may be <em>charged</em> to you as usage, unless your meter supports net metering.</li>
      <li><strong>Direction and shade.</strong> East- or west-facing balconies and shade from buildings cut output. The figures above assume south-facing panels with no shade.</li>
      <li><strong>The kit's real price.</strong> Prices change often. Kits that bundle a battery cost more and pay back more slowly unless you can shift power into the evening.</li>
      <li><strong>Size limits.</strong> Most state laws cap output at 1,200 W, and Colorado at 1,920 W. A kit over the cap is not covered by the law.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Is it legal where you live?</h2>
    <p>Savings mean little if the system isn't allowed. {rows.filter((r) => effectiveDate(r.s) && effectiveDate(r.s)! > BUILD_DATE).map((r) => `${r.s.name} (from ${fmtDate(effectiveDate(r.s)!)})`).join(', ')} have laws that are not in effect yet{rows.some((r) => r.s.verification === 'secondary_only') ? `, and ${rows.filter((r) => r.s.verification === 'secondary_only').map((r) => r.s.name).join(', ')} reportedly signed one that we are still verifying` : ''}. Several states require a specific safety certification, and no complete kit has shown us that certificate yet. <a href="/certification-watch/">See Certification watch</a>.</p>

    <h2>How we worked it out</h2>
    <p>Yearly savings = panel watts ÷ 1,000 × yearly output per kW × price. Payback = kit cost ÷ yearly savings.</p>
    <ul class="small">
      <li>Prices: {prices.source.title}, residential average for {prices.period}.</li>
      <li>Solar output: {yields.model}, run for each state capital with {yields.common.losses_pct}% system losses (<a href="https://developer.nlr.gov/docs/solar/pvwatts/v8/" rel="noopener">PVWatts documentation</a>).</li>
      <li>Share used at home: HTW Berlin, Stecker-Solar-Simulator: 800 W, no battery, vertical, facing south, German weather and household patterns. It gave {sc.runs.map((r) => `${r.pct}% at ${r.household_kwh.toLocaleString('en-US')} kWh/yr`).join(', ')} (<a href="https://solar.htw-berlin.de/rechner/stecker-solar-simulator/" rel="noopener">simulator</a>, <a href="https://solar.htw-berlin.de/wp-content/uploads/ORTH-2024-Stecker-Solar-Simulator-Dokumentation.pdf" rel="noopener">method</a>). Average US household use is 10,499 kWh/yr (EIA Form 861M).</li>
      <li>No incentives, no payment for exports, no change in electricity prices, and no inverter clipping.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="small muted">Numbers update automatically when we refresh prices or solar data. Last price update: {fmtDate(prices.last_checked)}.</p>
    <Disclaimer />
  </article>
</Base>
